lesson.body:
You already know how a for loop walks through a list, one element at a time. A dictionary can be looped over too, but the behavior is different in a way that trips a lot of beginners up: for x in some_dict does not give you the values, and it does not give you (key, value) pairs, it gives you only the keys, one at a time.

grades = {"Ana": 90, "Ben": 76, "Cy": 88}
for name in grades:
    print(name, grades[name])

This prints Ana 90, then Ben 76, then Cy 88. Each pass through the loop, name is bound to one key, and we use grades[name] to look up the matching value ourselves. This works, but it does an extra dictionary lookup on every iteration and reads a little clumsily.

The cleaner approach combines the for loop you already know with the items() method from the last chapter. Since each element of dictionary.items() is a (key, value) tuple, you can unpack it directly into two loop variables instead of one.

for name, grade in grades.items():
    if grade >= 80:
        print(f"{name} passed with {grade}")

This prints Ana passed with 90, then Cy passed with 88. Ben is skipped because his grade, 76, does not satisfy the condition grade >= 80. Notice there is no separate lookup step here: the moment name and grade are unpacked from the tuple, both pieces of information are already in hand.

As a rule of thumb: loop over a dictionary directly only when you truly need just the keys. The moment you need the value too, reach for .items() with two loop variables. It is shorter, it avoids a redundant lookup, and it makes your intent to use both the key and the value obvious to anyone reading the code.
